{"id": "main_v2_Q15", "raw_id": 15, "set": "main_v2", "type": "information_extraction", "hops": 1, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "What is the default maximum number of attempts for an outbound webhook job?", "expected_answer": "8 attempts.", "agent_id": "bc-ea36b989-a750-45ae-bc9b-c4642495a761", "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:25:25.309Z", "model_answer": "The default is **8** attempts.\n\nThat value is set in two places: the Mongoose schema for webhook jobs uses `default: 8` for `maxAttempts`, and `webhookService` uses `8` when no `maxAttempts` option is passed (`options.maxAttempts ?? 8`)."}
{"id": "main_v2_Q16", "raw_id": 16, "set": "main_v2", "type": "information_extraction", "hops": 1, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "How long are dead webhook jobs retained before the TTL index purges them?", "expected_answer": "30 days.", "agent_id": "bc-6bbe2137-0ea4-4d94-a9c7-837953fd9724", "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:26:29.641Z", "model_answer": "**30 days.**\n\nDead webhook jobs are removed by a MongoDB TTL index on `updatedAt` with `expireAfterSeconds: 60 * 60 * 24 * 30` (30 days), only for documents where `status` is `\"dead\"`. Purge timing is relative to that `updatedAt` value, not a separate \u201cdead at\u201d field.\n\n```50:54:src/models/WebhookJob.ts\n// ttl index - dead jobs get auto-purged after 30 days, we dont want mongo filling up with garbage\nWebhookJobSchema.index(\n  { updatedAt: 1 },\n  { expireAfterSeconds: 60 * 60 * 24 * 30, partialFilterExpression: { status: \"dead\" } }\n);\n```"}
{"id": "main_v2_Q17", "raw_id": 17, "set": "main_v2", "type": "information_extraction", "hops": 1, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "How often does the webhook worker poll for runnable jobs?", "expected_answer": "Every 5 seconds.", "agent_id": "bc-3210777f-4360-4883-ba83-06688b7172a7", "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:27:34.303Z", "model_answer": "The webhook worker is configured to run its poll loop on a **5 second** interval.\n\nThat comes from `POLL_INTERVAL_MS = 5_000` in `src/jobs/webhookWorker.ts`. Each poll is scheduled with `setTimeout(..., POLL_INTERVAL_MS)`, so the next run is queued **5 seconds** after `scheduleNextPoll` runs (after the previous `processWebhookBatch` finishes, including any work time for that batch)."}
{"id": "main_v2_Q18", "raw_id": 18, "set": "main_v2", "type": "information_extraction", "hops": 1, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "How many webhook jobs can one worker batch process at a time?", "expected_answer": "25 jobs.", "agent_id": "bc-cafd53d9-01b1-4189-9246-fd0b07593288", "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:28:08.185Z", "model_answer": "**25** \u2014 one worker run loads at most **25** webhook jobs per batch.\n\nThat comes from `BATCH_SIZE = 25` in `src/jobs/webhookWorker.ts`, which is applied to the query with `.limit(BATCH_SIZE)` when fetching `pending` / `failed` jobs whose `nextRunAt` is due."}
{"id": "main_v2_Q19", "raw_id": 19, "set": "main_v2", "type": "information_extraction", "hops": 1, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "Which HTTP status codes are treated as retryable by the outbound webhook dispatcher?", "expected_answer": "408, 429, 500, 502, 503, and 504.", "agent_id": "bc-965c54d6-d529-4087-b06e-17c9abeb85ae", "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:29:13.433Z", "model_answer": "In `src/webhooks/dispatcher.ts`, retryable **HTTP** statuses come from `RETRYABLE_STATUS_CODES`:\n\n**408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504**\n\nAnything in the 2xx range is treated as success. Other status codes (for example most 4xx besides 408 and 429) are treated as non-retryable. Separately, thrown errors (network/DNS/timeouts) are handled as retryable without a response status."}
{"id": "main_v2_Q21", "raw_id": 21, "set": "main_v2", "type": "information_extraction", "hops": 1, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "What is the current canonical RabbitMQ exchange name for downstream integrations after the migration?", "expected_answer": "order_stream", "agent_id": "bc-69ac7199-b5e4-4748-abbb-2da0bedcecc0", "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:29:48.634Z", "model_answer": "**`order_stream`**\n\nThe repo documents this explicitly: after the migration (T2), the canonical topic exchange for downstream integrations is **`order_stream`**. Before migration it was `order_events`; type (`topic`), durability, and routing keys (e.g. `order.created`) stayed the same\u2014only the exchange name changed. See `docs/integration/events-migration.md`, `docs/migrations/2026-03-12-order-stream-rollout.md`, and `docs/releases/2026-03-15-v1.6.0.md`."}
{"id": "main_v2_Q23", "raw_id": 23, "set": "main_v2", "type": "information_extraction", "hops": 1, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "Why does the tracing guide say OTLP HTTP was chosen over gRPC?", "expected_answer": "Because ALBs were dropping long-lived gRPC streams during scale-in events, while HTTP/1.1 payloads were more resilient in this infrastructure.", "agent_id": "bc-b13b4502-6941-49d2-a045-71f41ef87de9", "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:30:29.732Z", "model_answer": "From `docs/guides/tracing-implementation-notes.md`, the tracing guide says **OTLP HTTP** was chosen over **gRPC** because:\n\n**AWS ALBs were dropping long-lived gRPC streams during scale-in.** In that setup, **HTTP/1.1** export was more reliable, so traces were less likely to be lost under load. The tradeoff called out is slightly more payload overhead versus fewer dropped spans at peak."}
