{"id": "bug_pr_Q1", "raw_id": 1, "set": "bug_pr", "type": "debugging_and_provenance", "hops": 6, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "Why were webhook retries arriving too quickly in staging before v1.6.1?", "expected_answer": "Because the worker rescheduled retries using the stale stored attemptCount instead of the completed attempt number, so backoff lagged behind the real attempt progression until the completedAttemptCount fix shipped in v1.6.1.", "agent_id": null, "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:32:44.519711+00:00", "model_answer": null, "error": "400 Client Error: Bad Request for url: https://api.cursor.com/v0/agents"}
{"id": "bug_pr_Q2", "raw_id": 2, "set": "bug_pr", "type": "debugging_and_provenance", "hops": 6, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "What evidence shows the webhook retry bug was in attempt bookkeeping rather than in the jitter formula itself?", "expected_answer": "The retry investigation says the mismatch came from when an attempt was treated as completed, issue and PR #241 describe the stale-count bug, the worker code shows completedAttemptCount, and backoff.ts preserves the jitter formula rather than changing it.", "agent_id": null, "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:32:44.907280+00:00", "model_answer": null, "error": "400 Client Error: Bad Request for url: https://api.cursor.com/v0/agents"}
{"id": "bug_pr_Q3", "raw_id": 3, "set": "bug_pr", "type": "debugging_and_provenance", "hops": 6, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "Why can an edge proxy break Stripe webhook verification even if the forwarded JSON fields are identical?", "expected_answer": "Because Stripe verifies the original raw bytes, not a parsed JSON object. The proxy can re-serialize the body, changing the payload that stripe-signature covers, which is why the route keeps express.raw and the PR/review call out raw-body guardrails.", "agent_id": null, "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:32:45.325897+00:00", "model_answer": null, "error": "400 Client Error: Bad Request for url: https://api.cursor.com/v0/agents"}
{"id": "bug_pr_Q4", "raw_id": 4, "set": "bug_pr", "type": "debugging_and_provenance", "hops": 6, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "Which artifact chain distinguishes the PaymentIntent metadata-linking bug from the Stripe signature-verification bug?", "expected_answer": "Issue/PR/review #233 plus the webhook docs describe the missing metadata.orderId path where delivery can succeed but the order stays PENDING, while issue #87 and the staging incident describe the distinct bad-signature path that returns an explicit webhook error.", "agent_id": null, "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:32:46.804415+00:00", "model_answer": null, "error": "400 Client Error: Bad Request for url: https://api.cursor.com/v0/agents"}
{"id": "bug_pr_Q5", "raw_id": 5, "set": "bug_pr", "type": "debugging_and_provenance", "hops": 5, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "Why can a Stripe payment succeed while the order still remains PENDING with no signature error in the logs?", "expected_answer": "Because the missing metadata.orderId bug is a linkage failure, not a delivery failure: the webhook can be received successfully, but without the orderId it cannot map the payment back to an order and the order stays PENDING.", "agent_id": null, "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:32:47.196716+00:00", "model_answer": null, "error": "400 Client Error: Bad Request for url: https://api.cursor.com/v0/agents"}
{"id": "bug_pr_Q6", "raw_id": 6, "set": "bug_pr", "type": "debugging_and_provenance", "hops": 6, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "Why did the audit correlation hotfix improve traceability without making audit logging transactional?", "expected_answer": "Because the issue, PR #254, and its review explicitly frame the change as a defensive correlation-propagation patch, while the audit feature doc and audit service keep audit writes best-effort and non-blocking rather than required for business success.", "agent_id": null, "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:32:47.723294+00:00", "model_answer": null, "error": "400 Client Error: Bad Request for url: https://api.cursor.com/v0/agents"}
