{"id": "bug_pr_Q8", "raw_id": 8, "set": "bug_pr", "type": "debugging_and_provenance", "hops": 6, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "Why might a consumer with the correct routing key still miss order-created events after the exchange migration?", "expected_answer": "Because the migration changed the exchange name, not the routing key. The issue, PR, review, migration guide, release notes, and cutover runbook all show that a consumer still bound to order_events or a copied legacy internal name will miss traffic that now goes to order_stream.", "agent_id": null, "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:32:48.534981+00:00", "model_answer": null, "error": "400 Client Error: Bad Request for url: https://api.cursor.com/v0/agents"}
{"id": "bug_pr_Q9", "raw_id": 9, "set": "bug_pr", "type": "debugging_and_provenance", "hops": 6, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "Why did the RabbitMQ trace-gap investigation conclude that correlation IDs alone were insufficient?", "expected_answer": "Because correlation IDs only preserved request-level log traceability. The investigation, issue #142, tracing notes, PR #143, and publisher code show that end-to-end distributed traces across RabbitMQ required OpenTelemetry context injection into message headers.", "agent_id": null, "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:32:49.241291+00:00", "model_answer": null, "error": "400 Client Error: Bad Request for url: https://api.cursor.com/v0/agents"}
{"id": "bug_pr_Q10", "raw_id": 10, "set": "bug_pr", "type": "debugging_and_provenance", "hops": 5, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "Why does manual webhook replay preserve attemptCount instead of resetting delivery history?", "expected_answer": "Because the replay workflow is designed as an operational retry, not as a semantic reset. The runbook, admin route, PR/review #241, and WebhookJob model preserve attempt history so operators can replay a job without erasing provenance needed for debugging.", "agent_id": null, "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:32:49.648633+00:00", "model_answer": null, "error": "400 Client Error: Bad Request for url: https://api.cursor.com/v0/agents"}
{"id": "bug_pr_Q11", "raw_id": 11, "set": "bug_pr", "type": "debugging_and_provenance", "hops": 5, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "What design decision around the Stripe route is preserved in PR #87, and why is it important for future refactors?", "expected_answer": "PR #87 preserves two guardrails: keep express.raw on the Stripe route and do not add JWT auth there. Those constraints matter because raw-body handling is required for signature verification and adding auth is a separate but common webhook-breaking refactor mistake.", "agent_id": null, "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:32:50.078999+00:00", "model_answer": null, "error": "400 Client Error: Bad Request for url: https://api.cursor.com/v0/agents"}
{"id": "bug_pr_Q12", "raw_id": 12, "set": "bug_pr", "type": "debugging_and_provenance", "hops": 5, "leakage_risk": "LOW", "question": "What design decision did PR #233 deliberately not implement, and why?", "expected_answer": "PR #233 intentionally did not add server-side recovery for missing metadata.orderId. Instead it made the integration contract explicit so teams can diagnose the difference between a linkage bug and a webhook-signature bug without changing runtime behavior.", "agent_id": null, "created_at": "2026-03-29T20:32:50.569666+00:00", "model_answer": null, "error": "400 Client Error: Bad Request for url: https://api.cursor.com/v0/agents"}
